          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Casein Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-casein-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best casein protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein is the best all-round choice: about 24 g of micellar casein for roughly 120 calories per scoop, per the label, wide flavor choice and Informed Choice testing per US listings. Dymatize Elite Casein offers a slightly fuller dose, and NOW Sports Micellar Casein is the budget pick for tested athletes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is micellar casein better than calcium caseinate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For slow digestion, yes. Micellar casein keeps milk's natural protein structure, which clots in the stomach and releases amino acids over several hours, and it is the form used in most pre-sleep studies. Calcium caseinate is processed with acid, mixes more easily and suits bars and blends, but it is likely digested somewhat faster."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I take casein before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can help if you train in the evening, struggle to reach your daily protein target, or are older and benefit from an extra well-dosed feeding. Research supports 30 to 40 g about 30 to 60 minutes before sleep. If your daily protein is already high, the added benefit is small, because most of the effect comes from extra total protein."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is casein or whey better for building muscle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "When total daily protein is matched, long-term studies mostly show similar gains in muscle and strength. Whey causes a faster, bigger short-term spike in muscle protein synthesis and carries a little more leucine; casein gives a slower, longer release. Buy whey first for value and convenience, and add casein for overnight or long gaps if you want."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will casein before bed make me gain fat?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not by itself. A casein shake adds roughly 120 to 150 calories, which count toward your daily total like any other food. Studies in active people have not found pre-sleep casein blunting overnight fat burning. If you are in a calorie deficit and stay there, a bedtime shake will not stop fat loss, and it may curb late-night snacking."
          }
