    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Casein is the slow half of milk protein. It clots in the stomach and trickles amino acids into the blood for hours, which makes it the natural choice for a shake before bed, a long gap between meals or a thick protein pudding. It is not a better muscle builder than whey, and nobody needs it if their daily protein is already covered. But if you want one of those jobs done well, the best casein protein powder is a micellar casein with an honest label, a credible certification and a texture you can live with. These five clear that bar.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-is-casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Casein Is and Why It Digests Slowly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cow's milk protein is roughly 80 percent casein and 20 percent whey. Both are complete proteins with every essential amino acid, and both are high-quality by any scoring system. The difference that matters is how they behave once you swallow them. Whey stays liquid in the stomach, empties quickly and floods the blood with amino acids within an hour or so. Casein meets stomach acid and clots into a soft gel, which empties slowly and releases its amino acids over several hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            That contrast was measured directly in a 1997 study that coined the terms fast and slow protein. Whey produced a rapid, high and short-lived rise in blood amino acids. Casein produced a lower rise that lasted much longer, and it suppressed whole-body protein breakdown in a way whey did not. Over the seven-hour test period, casein came out ahead on whole-body leucine balance, while whey was better at driving a quick burst of protein synthesis. Neither result means one protein is better overall. It means they are tools for different jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein's job is coverage. It suits any stretch where you would like a slow drip of amino acids rather than a spike: overnight, a long workday without a proper lunch, or a flight. Its slower digestion also makes it more filling for many people, and its tendency to thicken means it behaves more like a food than a drink when mixed with little liquid. That is why casein shows up in pre-sleep research, in dieting advice for evening hunger and in protein pudding recipes.
          </p>

          <p>
            What casein does not do is build more muscle than whey when your total protein for the day is the same. For most people, a casein tub is a second tub, bought because it fills a gap that whey fills badly. If you are deciding which single protein to buy first, start with our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings, and if you want the full map of protein types, our guide to the <a href="/types-of-protein-powder/">types of protein powder</a> sets casein alongside whey, egg, soy and plant blends.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Casein is slow protein, not superior protein.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It clots in the stomach and releases amino acids for hours, which makes it useful before bed and across long gaps. When daily protein is matched, it builds muscle about as well as whey, not better.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, taste panels or a sleep study for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, published third-party certifications and the research on casein and pre-sleep protein, which is the method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. When we mention taste, thickness or mixing, we are passing on what buyers and reviewers widely report, not a result of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was the type of casein. Every pick is micellar casein, the form that keeps milk's natural protein structure and digests most slowly. Products built mainly on calcium caseinate, or blends that list casein after several faster proteins, did not qualify, because the slow release is the whole reason to buy casein. The second filter was disclosure: each product had to state its protein per serving plainly, with no proprietary blend and no added free amino acids such as glycine or taurine that can inflate a protein reading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next came dose and density. We looked for roughly 20 to 26 g of protein per serving with most of the calories coming from protein, and we flagged where a serving is two scoops rather than one, since that halves how long a tub lasts. Then we weighted third-party testing. Banned-substance certifications from Informed Sport, Informed Choice or NSF Certified for Sport do not make a protein taste better, but they are the only independent check most buyers can verify, and they matter to anyone in a drug-tested sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we considered how people actually use casein. A shake before bed, a pudding in a bowl and a scoop stirred into oats have different needs, so we kept one unflavored option for cooking and leaned toward flavored tubs that buyers consistently say they finish. Price mattered only as a tiebreaker; we describe cost in relative terms because tub prices move week to week.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
