        <div id="micellar-vs-caseinate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Micellar Casein vs Calcium Caseinate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein on a label comes in a few forms, and they are not interchangeable. Micellar casein is made by filtering skim milk through fine membranes, which separates the casein while leaving its natural structure intact. In milk, casein exists as micelles, tiny clusters of protein held together with calcium and phosphate. Keeping those micelles whole is what lets micellar casein clot in the stomach and digest slowly, and it is the form used in most of the pre-sleep research.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calcium caseinate is made differently. The casein is precipitated out of milk with acid, which breaks up the micelles, and then neutralized with a calcium compound so it dissolves again. The result is cheaper, mixes more easily and has a cleaner, less chalky texture, which is why it shows up in protein bars, ready-to-drink shakes and budget blends. It still contains casein's amino acids and is still a good protein. It just loses some of the structure that makes micellar casein slow, and the limited comparative evidence suggests caseinate is digested somewhat faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also see sodium caseinate, which is similar to calcium caseinate, and hydrolyzed casein, which has been pre-digested into smaller peptides. Hydrolyzed casein digests quickly, roughly like whey, which defeats the purpose if you wanted slow protein. Milk protein concentrate and milk protein isolate contain both casein and whey in roughly milk's natural ratio, so they sit in the middle: slower than whey, faster than pure micellar casein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. If you are buying casein for slow release, the first protein on the ingredient list should be micellar casein. If a tub labelled casein lists calcium caseinate first, or lists a whey and casein blend, it may be a perfectly good protein but it is not what the overnight research tested. All five of our picks list micellar casein as their protein source.
          </p>

