        <div id="casein-before-bed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Casein Before Bed: What the Research Actually Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pre-sleep protein is the reason most people buy casein, so it is worth being precise about the evidence. The idea is that sleep is the longest fast of the day, and a slow protein taken shortly before bed could keep amino acids available overnight, when muscle repair continues. The key early study, published in 2012, had young men lift weights in the evening and then drink 40 g of casein or a placebo before sleep. The casein was digested and absorbed normally during the night, whole-body protein balance improved, and overnight muscle protein synthesis came out roughly a fifth higher than with placebo.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later work extended the finding. A study in older men found that 40 g of casein before bed raised overnight muscle protein synthesis while 20 g did not, which fits the broader evidence that older muscle needs a larger dose to respond. A 12-week training study in young men found that a nightly drink with about 27.5 g of casein produced greater gains in strength and muscle size than a non-caloric placebo. The International Society of Sports Nutrition's position on nutrient timing concluded that 30 to 40 g of casein before sleep can increase overnight muscle protein synthesis and metabolic rate without harming fat breakdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the important caveat. In most of those trials, the placebo group simply ate less protein, so the casein group won partly because it got more protein in total. Reviews that have pooled the pre-sleep trials generally conclude that the benefit is largely explained by that extra protein, and when total daily protein is matched, a pre-sleep feeding has not shown a clear advantage over eating the same protein earlier in the day. Timing appears to be a small refinement on top of the basics, not a lever on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            So who should bother? Pre-sleep casein makes the most sense for people who struggle to hit their daily protein target, for lifters who train in the evening, for older adults who benefit from an extra well-dosed protein feeding, and for anyone whose dinner is early and light. If you already eat enough protein spread across three or four meals, a bedtime shake is optional. Our explainer on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers the rest of the timing evidence, including the anabolic window.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST TAKE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Casein before bed helps mostly because it adds protein.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Overnight muscle protein synthesis rises with 30-40 g of casein before sleep, but when total daily protein is matched the long-term advantage largely disappears. Treat it as a convenient way to reach your target, not a secret.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="casein-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Casein vs Whey: Which Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can buy only one protein, buy whey. It costs less per gram of protein in most cases, it mixes thin in water, it comes in more flavors, and it carries slightly more leucine, the amino acid most associated with switching on muscle protein synthesis. Whey is roughly 10 to 11 percent leucine; casein is closer to 8 or 9 percent. A 25 g scoop of whey clears the roughly 2 to 3 g leucine threshold most researchers point to, and a 25 g serving of casein is close to it, which is one reason casein doses in research tend to be larger.
          </p>

          <p>
            In head-to-head comparisons after a workout, whey produces a bigger short-term spike in muscle protein synthesis, while casein produces a smaller but longer response. Measured over several hours, net protein balance after the two is often similar. Longer training studies that compare whey and casein directly mostly find similar gains in muscle and strength when total protein is matched. The differences people argue about online tend to vanish once you account for how much protein each group actually ate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where casein genuinely wins is in situations where slow matters. Before bed, before a long gap between meals, or as an evening snack when you are dieting and hungry, casein's slower digestion and thicker texture are features. Some people also find casein more filling than whey at the same calories, which can help when appetite is the problem. Where whey wins is after training, in the morning, in coffee, and any time you want a quick, light drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plenty of lifters keep both: whey during the day and casein at night. That is reasonable if the budget allows, but it is not required. A glass of milk, a bowl of Greek yogurt or a serving of cottage cheese is mostly casein too, and does the same overnight job at a lower cost per gram for many shoppers. If you need a lactose-light option, our comparison of <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explains why isolates are gentler, and casein generally carries a little more lactose than an isolate.
          </p>

